{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_function_to6xb40qeek5_1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Go Enum Type Pattern\n2\t\n3\tThis guide documents the canonical string-based enum pattern used throughout Benjamin Borbe's Go projects - a widely used pattern (50+ implementations across projects).\n4\t\n5\t## When to Use This Pattern\n6\t\n7\tUse this pattern when you need:\n8\t- **Type-safe constants** with validation against a known set of values\n9\t- **Compile-time guarantees** instead of runtime string matching\n10\t- **Collection operations** (Contains, filtering, etc.)\n11\t- **Database/JSON serialization** of enumerated values\n12\t\n13\t**Don't use** for simple value wrappers that don't need predefined constants (e.g., user IDs, arbitrary names).\n14\t\n15\t## Core Pattern Structure\n16\t\n17\t### RULE go-enum-type/typed-constants-with-collection (MUST)\n18\t\n19\t**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n20\t**Applies when**: a Go package introduces a finite set of enum-like values (status codes, kinds, modes, etc.) declared as untyped `const` strings or `const` ints, without a paired typed `string`/`int` newtype AND without an `Available<Name>s` collection containing every valid value.\n21\t**Enforcement**: `rules/go/typed-constants-with-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags untyped `const` string specs) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm shared semantic prefix and check whether the package already has a typed newtype + `Available*` collection.\n22\t**Why**: Untyped enum constants spread through the codebase as bare strings — every call site can pass any string, every comparison can typo, and the linter has no signal that \"scheduled\" / \"completed\" / \"pending\" are meant to be members of a closed set. Typed constants paired with an `AvailableXs` collection turn the enum into a first-class type the type-checker enforces and that `Validate()` can range over. The `Available*` collection is what makes the pattern self-describing — a new contributor reads the package and immediately sees the full value space.\n23\t\n24\t#### Bad\n25\t\n26\t```go\n27\t// Untyped string constants — every call site can pass any string\n28\tconst (\n29\t\tOrderStatusPending    = \"pending\"\n30\t\tOrderStatusProcessing = \"processing\"\n31\t\tOrderStatusCompleted  = \"completed\"\n32\t\tOrderStatusFailed     = \"failed\"\n33\t)\n34\t\n35\tfunc Process(status string) error { // accepts any string; typo silent\n36\t\tif status == \"compleated\" { ... } // compiles cleanly; runtime no-op\n37\t}\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t#### Good\n41\t\n42\t```go\n43\t// OrderStatus is the closed set of lifecycle states an Order may occupy.\n44\ttype OrderStatus string\n45\t\n46\t// Recognised OrderStatus values. The Available* collection below mirrors\n47\t// this set — keep both in sync.\n48\tconst (\n49\t\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n50\t\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n51\t\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n52\t\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n53\t)\n54\t\n55\t// OrderStatuses is a collection of OrderStatus values, used by Validate to\n56\t// check membership in the closed set.\n57\ttype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n58\t\n59\t// AvailableOrderStatuses lists every OrderStatus value the system accepts.\n60\t// Validate() ranges over this collection; tests and dispatchers iterate it.\n61\tvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n62\t\tPendingOrderStatus,\n63\t\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n64\t\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n65\t\tFailedOrderStatus,\n66\t}\n67\t\n68\t// Process advances an order to the next state for the given status.\n69\t// Returns an error if the status is unknown or the order is in a terminal state.\n70\tfunc Process(ctx context.Context, status OrderStatus) error { // type-checked at call site\n71\t\t// ...\n72\t}\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\t### Minimal Complete Implementation\n76\t\n77\t```go\n78\tpackage order\n79\t\n80\timport (\n81\t\t\"context\"\n82\t\n83\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/collection\"\n84\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/errors\"\n85\t\t\"github.com/bborbe/validation\"\n86\t)\n87\t\n88\t// 1. Define constants with explicit typing\n89\tconst (\n90\t\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n91\t\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n92\t\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n93\t\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n94\t)\n95\t\n96\t// 2. Declare available values collection\n97\tvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n98\t\tPendingOrderStatus,\n99\t\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n100\t\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n101\t\tFailedOrderStatus,\n102\t}\n103\t\n104\t// 3. Define singular type\n105\ttype OrderStatus string\n106\t\n107\t// 4. Implement String() method\n108\tfunc (o OrderStatus) String() string {\n109\t\treturn string(o)\n110\t}\n111\t\n112\t// 5. Implement Validate() method checking against Available* collection\n113\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n114\t\tif AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) == false {\n115\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n116\t\t}\n117\t\treturn nil\n118\t}\n119\t\n120\t// 6. Define plural collection type\n121\ttype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n122\t\n123\t// 7. Implement Contains() method on collection\n124\tfunc (o OrderStatuses) Contains(status OrderStatus) bool {\n125\t\treturn collection.Contains(o, status)\n126\t}\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t### RULE go-enum-type/validate-against-available-collection (MUST)\n130\t\n131\t**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n132\t**Applies when**: a Go enum type's `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method validates against an inline switch / hardcoded value list / regex, instead of `AvailableXs.Contains(value)`.\n133\t**Enforcement**: `rules/go/validate-against-available-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags `Validate` methods containing an inline `switch`) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm the switch is enum-membership validation and the package has an `Available*` collection.\n134\t**Why**: Validating against an inline switch duplicates the enum's value space — adding a new enum constant requires updating both `const (...)` and the `Validate()` body, and the type checker can't enforce the pair. Range-over-`AvailableXs` collapses the two into one declaration: the collection IS the validation source, so the only place to add a value is the collection literal. Adds-a-constant-but-forgets-to-update-validate becomes structurally impossible.\n135\t\n136\t#### Bad\n137\t\n138\t```go\n139\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n140\t\tswitch o {\n141\t\tcase PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus,\n142\t\t     CompletedOrderStatus, FailedOrderStatus:\n143\t\t\treturn nil\n144\t\tdefault:\n145\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n146\t\t}\n147\t\t// Adding a new status to const(...) without updating this switch\n148\t\t// produces silent validation failures.\n149\t}\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\t#### Good\n153\t\n154\t```go\n155\tfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n156\t\tif !AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) {\n157\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n158\t\t}\n159\t\treturn nil\n160\t}\n161\t// Adding a new status to AvailableOrderStatuses automatically extends\n162\t// the validation surface. Single source of truth.\n163\t```\n164\t\n165\t## Implementation Checklist\n166\t\n167\tWhen creating a new enum type, ensure you have:\n168\t\n169\t- [ ] Constants with explicit type annotation (`const Name Type = \"value\"`)\n170\t- [ ] `var AvailableXs` collection containing all valid values\n171\t- [ ] Singular type definition (`type Status string`)\n172\t- [ ] `String() string` method on singular type\n173\t- [ ] `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method checking against `AvailableXs`\n174\t- [ ] Plural collection type (`type Statuses []Status`)\n175\t- [ ] `Contains()` method on plural type using `github.com/bborbe/collection`\n176\t\n177\t## Usage Example\n178\t\n179\t```go\n180\ttype Order struct {\n181\t\tID       string      `json:\"id\"`\n182\t\tStatus   OrderStatus `json:\"status\"`\n183\t\tPriority Priority    `json:\"priority\"`\n184\t}\n185\t\n186\tfunc (o Order) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n187\t\treturn validation.Validate(ctx, o.Status, o.Priority)\n188\t}\n189\t\n190\tfunc ProcessOrder(ctx context.Context, order Order) error {\n191\t\t// Type-safe comparison\n192\t\tif order.Status == CompletedOrderStatus {\n193\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"order already completed\")\n194\t\t}\n195\t\n196\t\t// Collection operations\n197\t\tactiveStatuses := OrderStatuses{PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus}\n198\t\tif !activeStatuses.Contains(order.Status) {\n199\t\t\treturn errors.New(\"order not in active state\")\n200\t\t}\n201\t\n202\t\t// String conversion\n203\t\tfmt.Printf(\"Processing order with status: %s\\n\", order.Status.String())\n204\t\n205\t\t// Validation\n206\t\tif err := order.Validate(ctx); err != nil {\n207\t\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"invalid order\")\n208\t\t}\n209\t\n210\t\treturn nil\n211\t}\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\tSee [Go Validation Framework Guide](go-validation-framework-guide.md) for validation patterns.\n215\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ff1ffc3-cf34-4911-8715-5346eb85863e","uuid":"fe814283-8d68-42ba-8ccd-f939b9ee7112","timestamp":"2026-08-15T08:21:57.633Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/go-enum-type-pattern.md","content":"# Go Enum Type Pattern\n\nThis guide documents the canonical string-based enum pattern used throughout Benjamin Borbe's Go projects - a widely used pattern (50+ implementations across projects).\n\n## When to Use This Pattern\n\nUse this pattern when you need:\n- **Type-safe constants** with validation against a known set of values\n- **Compile-time guarantees** instead of runtime string matching\n- **Collection operations** (Contains, filtering, etc.)\n- **Database/JSON serialization** of enumerated values\n\n**Don't use** for simple value wrappers that don't need predefined constants (e.g., user IDs, arbitrary names).\n\n## Core Pattern Structure\n\n### RULE go-enum-type/typed-constants-with-collection (MUST)\n\n**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n**Applies when**: a Go package introduces a finite set of enum-like values (status codes, kinds, modes, etc.) declared as untyped `const` strings or `const` ints, without a paired typed `string`/`int` newtype AND without an `Available<Name>s` collection containing every valid value.\n**Enforcement**: `rules/go/typed-constants-with-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags untyped `const` string specs) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm shared semantic prefix and check whether the package already has a typed newtype + `Available*` collection.\n**Why**: Untyped enum constants spread through the codebase as bare strings — every call site can pass any string, every comparison can typo, and the linter has no signal that \"scheduled\" / \"completed\" / \"pending\" are meant to be members of a closed set. Typed constants paired with an `AvailableXs` collection turn the enum into a first-class type the type-checker enforces and that `Validate()` can range over. The `Available*` collection is what makes the pattern self-describing — a new contributor reads the package and immediately sees the full value space.\n\n#### Bad\n\n```go\n// Untyped string constants — every call site can pass any string\nconst (\n\tOrderStatusPending    = \"pending\"\n\tOrderStatusProcessing = \"processing\"\n\tOrderStatusCompleted  = \"completed\"\n\tOrderStatusFailed     = \"failed\"\n)\n\nfunc Process(status string) error { // accepts any string; typo silent\n\tif status == \"compleated\" { ... } // compiles cleanly; runtime no-op\n}\n```\n\n#### Good\n\n```go\n// OrderStatus is the closed set of lifecycle states an Order may occupy.\ntype OrderStatus string\n\n// Recognised OrderStatus values. The Available* collection below mirrors\n// this set — keep both in sync.\nconst (\n\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n)\n\n// OrderStatuses is a collection of OrderStatus values, used by Validate to\n// check membership in the closed set.\ntype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n\n// AvailableOrderStatuses lists every OrderStatus value the system accepts.\n// Validate() ranges over this collection; tests and dispatchers iterate it.\nvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n\tPendingOrderStatus,\n\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n\tFailedOrderStatus,\n}\n\n// Process advances an order to the next state for the given status.\n// Returns an error if the status is unknown or the order is in a terminal state.\nfunc Process(ctx context.Context, status OrderStatus) error { // type-checked at call site\n\t// ...\n}\n```\n\n### Minimal Complete Implementation\n\n```go\npackage order\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\n\t\"github.com/bborbe/collection\"\n\t\"github.com/bborbe/errors\"\n\t\"github.com/bborbe/validation\"\n)\n\n// 1. Define constants with explicit typing\nconst (\n\tPendingOrderStatus    OrderStatus = \"pending\"\n\tProcessingOrderStatus OrderStatus = \"processing\"\n\tCompletedOrderStatus  OrderStatus = \"completed\"\n\tFailedOrderStatus     OrderStatus = \"failed\"\n)\n\n// 2. Declare available values collection\nvar AvailableOrderStatuses = OrderStatuses{\n\tPendingOrderStatus,\n\tProcessingOrderStatus,\n\tCompletedOrderStatus,\n\tFailedOrderStatus,\n}\n\n// 3. Define singular type\ntype OrderStatus string\n\n// 4. Implement String() method\nfunc (o OrderStatus) String() string {\n\treturn string(o)\n}\n\n// 5. Implement Validate() method checking against Available* collection\nfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n\tif AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) == false {\n\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// 6. Define plural collection type\ntype OrderStatuses []OrderStatus\n\n// 7. Implement Contains() method on collection\nfunc (o OrderStatuses) Contains(status OrderStatus) bool {\n\treturn collection.Contains(o, status)\n}\n```\n\n### RULE go-enum-type/validate-against-available-collection (MUST)\n\n**Owner**: go-architecture-assistant\n**Applies when**: a Go enum type's `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method validates against an inline switch / hardcoded value list / regex, instead of `AvailableXs.Contains(value)`.\n**Enforcement**: `rules/go/validate-against-available-collection.yml` (mechanical first-pass flags `Validate` methods containing an inline `switch`) + judgment-tier LLM adjudication to confirm the switch is enum-membership validation and the package has an `Available*` collection.\n**Why**: Validating against an inline switch duplicates the enum's value space — adding a new enum constant requires updating both `const (...)` and the `Validate()` body, and the type checker can't enforce the pair. Range-over-`AvailableXs` collapses the two into one declaration: the collection IS the validation source, so the only place to add a value is the collection literal. Adds-a-constant-but-forgets-to-update-validate becomes structurally impossible.\n\n#### Bad\n\n```go\nfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n\tswitch o {\n\tcase PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus,\n\t     CompletedOrderStatus, FailedOrderStatus:\n\t\treturn nil\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n\t}\n\t// Adding a new status to const(...) without updating this switch\n\t// produces silent validation failures.\n}\n```\n\n#### Good\n\n```go\nfunc (o OrderStatus) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n\tif !AvailableOrderStatuses.Contains(o) {\n\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, validation.Error, \"unknown order status '%s'\", o)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n// Adding a new status to AvailableOrderStatuses automatically extends\n// the validation surface. Single source of truth.\n```\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n\nWhen creating a new enum type, ensure you have:\n\n- [ ] Constants with explicit type annotation (`const Name Type = \"value\"`)\n- [ ] `var AvailableXs` collection containing all valid values\n- [ ] Singular type definition (`type Status string`)\n- [ ] `String() string` method on singular type\n- [ ] `Validate(ctx context.Context) error` method checking against `AvailableXs`\n- [ ] Plural collection type (`type Statuses []Status`)\n- [ ] `Contains()` method on plural type using `github.com/bborbe/collection`\n\n## Usage Example\n\n```go\ntype Order struct {\n\tID       string      `json:\"id\"`\n\tStatus   OrderStatus `json:\"status\"`\n\tPriority Priority    `json:\"priority\"`\n}\n\nfunc (o Order) Validate(ctx context.Context) error {\n\treturn validation.Validate(ctx, o.Status, o.Priority)\n}\n\nfunc ProcessOrder(ctx context.Context, order Order) error {\n\t// Type-safe comparison\n\tif order.Status == CompletedOrderStatus {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"order already completed\")\n\t}\n\n\t// Collection operations\n\tactiveStatuses := OrderStatuses{PendingOrderStatus, ProcessingOrderStatus}\n\tif !activeStatuses.Contains(order.Status) {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"order not in active state\")\n\t}\n\n\t// String conversion\n\tfmt.Printf(\"Processing order with status: %s\\n\", order.Status.String())\n\n\t// Validation\n\tif err := order.Validate(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\treturn errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, \"invalid order\")\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n```\n\nSee [Go Validation Framework Guide](go-validation-framework-guide.md) for validation patterns.\n","numLines":215,"startLine":1,"totalLines":215}}}
